<h2>Two decrees, two appeals, one outcome</h2>
<p>The Family Court at Jaisalmer had decided the husband\'s divorce petition under Section 13(1)(ia) and the connected petition under Section 9 together, granting divorce on the ground of cruelty and declining restitution. The wife appealed both results. The Division Bench examined the record — including the husband\'s consistent testimony that conjugal relations ceased within weeks of the marriage, the exhibited suicide note, and the wife\'s own admissions in cross-examination — and dismissed both appeals, affirming the trial court\'s findings as grounded in evidence and law.</p>

<h2>How the cruelty finding was built</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Pleaded conduct</strong> — the husband alleged refusal of physical relations since late October 2018, taunts about his kulfi business, demands connected with the wife\'s salary as a Junior Accountant, and threats of self-harm.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Corroboration</strong> — a suicide note was exhibited, and the husband\'s version of the cessation of relations went effectively unrebutted; the wife identified no physical incapacity or justifying reason.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Admissions</strong> — the wife accepted there had been no major quarrel after the marriage and expressed willingness to cohabit, but only outside Balotra, underscoring that the separation was a matter of unilateral stance rather than of the husband\'s misconduct.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Legal standard</strong> — applying Samar Ghosh and 2026 INSC 620, the Bench held that sustained unilateral denial without valid reason, aggravated by suicide threats, satisfies the mental cruelty threshold of Section 13(1)(ia).</div>
</div>

<h2>The suicide-threat dimension</h2>
<p>Threats of suicide occupy a distinct place in cruelty jurisprudence. They weaponise the criminal consequences that follow an unnatural death in a matrimonial home, and courts have repeatedly treated them as conduct no spouse should be compelled to live under. Here the threat was not a stray allegation: a note existed on the record. Combined with the denial of conjugal life, the cumulative picture satisfied the settled test that mental cruelty must make it unreasonable to expect the parties to live together — a standard that looks to the totality of conduct rather than to isolated incidents.</p>

<h2>What the decision signals for matrimonial trials</h2>
<p>Three practice points emerge. First, cross-examination remains decisive in cruelty trials: the wife\'s admissions — no quarrel, no incapacity, willingness to cohabit only on her own locational terms — did more work than any affirmative evidence. Second, a condition of cohabitation that one spouse attaches unilaterally does not answer a cruelty case; it can reinforce it, because it frames the separation as chosen rather than forced. Third, appellate interference in matrimonial decrees requires perversity or misapplication of law; where the Family Court\'s findings rest on admissions and documents, a Division Bench will rarely re-weigh them. For parties on either side of a contested divorce, the case is a reminder that these matters are won on the record built at trial, not on appeal.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The judgment concerns the fault ground of cruelty under the Hindu Marriage Act. It does not alter the position that a decree must rest on pleaded and proved conduct; nor does it make every refusal of intimacy actionable. Spouses facing such allegations should ensure their explanation — medical, circumstantial or arising from the other side\'s conduct — is pleaded and proved, since silence on justification is what proved fatal here.</p>
</div>
